"confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill expands litigation procedures, jurisdictional reach, and limits defenses in civil claims, directly affecting law firms handling art recovery and international litigation."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Museums and cultural institutions holding artwork are subject to expanded liability and jurisdictional reach under the modified claims procedures."}],"plainSummary":"This bill removes the 2026 deadline for filing lawsuits to recover art and property stolen during the Holocaust, allowing claims to be filed indefinitely as long as they are brought within six years of discovering the item. It makes it easier to sue foreign governments in U.S. courts and to serve legal papers on defendants anywhere in the country. The bill also blocks certain legal defenses, such as arguments that too much time has passed or that U.S. courts should defer to foreign laws, for both current and future cases.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People who have been the victim of a crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"N000002","score":0.032950606363763804},"overlapCount":28},"meaning":{"percentile":42.70389651364568,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7507326509255339}},{"billId":"119-hr-51","bioguideId":"M001217","cycle":2026,"score":0.008225440985540898,"matches":[{"industry":"real-estate","donorAmount":18000,"donorShare":0.01391997705245383,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.004429083607598945},{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":18000,"donorShare":0.01391997705245383,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.003796357377941953}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7507326509255339},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-51","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"51","title":"Washington, D.C. Admission Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Oversight and Government Reform, and in addition to the Committees on Rules, Armed Services, the Judiciary, and Energy and Commerce, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":["Congressional districts and representation","Congressional elections","Constitution and constitutional amendments","District of Columbia","Elections, voting, political campaign regulation","Government buildings, facilities, and property","House of Representatives","Legislative rules and procedure","Members of Congress","Presidents and presidential powers, Vice Presidents","State and local government operations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"N000147","cosponsorCount":204,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/51","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill fundamentally restructures the governance of the District, creating a new state government and redefining the federal district."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Statehood would change property tax authority and land-use regulation for most of the current District, affecting developers and property owners."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The transition of DC courts, judicial proceedings, and contractual obligations to state authority would affect legal practice and litigation in the new jurisdiction."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would admit most of the current District of Columbia as the 51st state, called Washington, Douglass Commonwealth, with two senators and one representative in Congress. A small federal district containing the Capitol, White House, Supreme Court, and nearby monuments would remain as the seat of the federal government. The bill sets up a transition process for DC laws, courts, contracts, and obligations to continue under state authority, and creates a commission to manage the shift to statehood.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who votes.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001245","score":0.050448841212731466},"overlapCount":197},"meaning":{"percentile":39.81700583688279,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7507326509255339}},{"billId":"119-hr-1810","bioguideId":"M001217","cycle":2026,"score":0.0081199866139314,"matches":[{"industry":"education","donorAmount":10500,"donorShare":0.0081199866139314,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0081199866139314}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7507326509255339},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1810","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1810","title":"Safe Schools Improvement Act","introducedDate":"2025-03-03","latestActionDate":"2025-03-03","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Education and Workforce.","policyArea":"Education","subjects":["Assault and harassment offenses","Census and government statistics","Child safety and welfare","Congressional oversight","Data collection, sharing, protection","Disability and paralysis","Education programs funding","Elementary and secondary education","Government information and archives","Government studies and investigations","Racial and ethnic relations","Religion"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001156","cosponsorCount":188,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1810","industries":[{"industry":"education","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill imposes new policy, reporting, and grievance-procedure obligations on local educational agencies and state education systems."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require states to ensure that local school districts adopt policies prohibiting bullying and harassment of students based on race, color, national origin, disability, religion, sex (including sexual orientation, gender identity, and intersex traits). School districts would need to notify students and parents annually about these policies, establish complaint procedures, and publicly report data on bullying incidents. The Department of Education would evaluate anti-bullying programs every two years, and the National Center for Education Statistics would collect nationwide data on these incidents.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"School districts, and children in them. Children, and the adults responsible for them.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M000687","score":0.05798866766035882},"overlapCount":178},"meaning":{"percentile":39.233317557974445,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7507326509255339}},{"billId":"119-hr-9944","bioguideId":"M001217","cycle":2026,"score":0.007617320775926123,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":18000,"donorShare":0.01391997705245383,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.00487199196835884},{"industry":"agriculture","donorAmount":9000,"donorShare":0.006959988526226915,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0017399971315567287},{"industry":"hospitality","donorAmount":5500,"donorShare":0.004253326321583114,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.0008506652643166229},{"industry":"construction","donorAmount":1000,"donorShare":0.0007733320584696571,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.00015466641169393144}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7507326509255339},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-9944","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"9944","title":"To authorize judicial review of temporary protected status terminations, and for other purposes.","introducedDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on the Judiciary, and in addition to the Committee on Rules, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"W000797","cosponsorCount":62,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/9944","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The bill creates new opportunities for judicial review, which would generate litigation work for immigration attorneys."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Agricultural employers rely on TPS holders as workers; changes to termination procedures could affect labor availability in this sector."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Hotels and restaurants employ TPS holders; judicial review of terminations could affect workforce stability in these industries."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Construction employers use TPS holders as workers; the bill's review mechanism could affect labor supply predictability."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would allow people to challenge in court when the government ends Temporary Protected Status (TPS) for their country. TPS is a designation that lets people from certain countries stay in the U.S. temporarily when their home country faces armed conflict, natural disaster, or other extraordinary conditions. Currently, decisions to terminate TPS are not subject to judicial review; this bill would change that.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"We cannot tell from the labels on this bill who it reaches. The subject is too broad to say.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"C001091","score":0.04439139830557925},"overlapCount":61},"meaning":{"percentile":36.9458905190093,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7507326509255339}},{"billId":"119-hr-1657","bioguideId":"M001217","cycle":2026,"score":0.007449144346343264,"matches":[{"industry":"retail-consumer","donorAmount":20500,"donorShare":0.015853307198627972,"billConfidence":0.48750000000000004,"contribution":0.007449144346343264}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"keyword-fallback","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7507326509255339},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1657","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1657","title":"Humane Cosmetics Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Energy and Commerce.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Animal protection and human-animal relationships","Civil actions and liability","Computers and information technology","Cosmetics and personal care","Drug safety, medical device, and laboratory regulation","Federal preemption","Government information and archives","Manufacturing","Retail and wholesale trades"],"sponsorBioguideId":"B001292","cosponsorCount":145,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1657","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Keyword match on \"medical device\" in the bill text. No LLM ke